# Security Policy

## Supported versions

Only the latest release receives security fixes.

## Reporting a vulnerability

Do not open a public issue.
{% if author_email -%}
Email {{ author_email }} with details and, if possible, steps to reproduce.
{%- else -%}
Use [GitHub private vulnerability reporting]({{ repo_url }}/security/advisories/new)
to report privately.
{%- endif %}

You should get an acknowledgement within a few business days.

## Handling secrets

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in real values there. `.env` is
gitignored and never committed; `.env.example` must keep placeholders only --
never a real, plausible, or generated value.

The ignore rules also cover `.env.*`, common private-key extensions
(`*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.keystore`), and conventional SSH/token
filenames (`id_rsa`, `credentials.json`). A local `detect-private-key`
pre-commit hook blocks committing a recognisable private key. Ignore rules and
that hook are the last line of defence, not detection: they do not scan
history or catch secrets embedded in code or logs.

For broader coverage, add an optional secret-scanning tool (for example,
[gitleaks](https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks)) to your own pre-commit
config, or enable your Git host's native scanning (for example, GitHub push
protection / secret scanning under repository settings). Neither is enabled
by this template.
